' AVALANCHE RUN
' A first-person downhill race for the ColecoVision, in Amy, by Amy Bienvenu.
' Inspired by AVALANCHE in Tim Hartnell's Giant Book of Computer Games
' (Ballantine Books, 1984; Interface Publications, 1983), where the mayor
' of an alpine village prepares for the avalanches. Here the avalanche has
' already broken loose above you: ski down ahead of it and warn the
' village before it buries you.
'
' Three stages, each a run down to a village. The snow rushes toward you
' in perspective; the piste bends left and right between walls of forest.
' Trees and rocks on the piste throw you over if you hit them; skiing
' between a pair of flags (a gate) scores and gives a little speed. The
' avalanche is behind you: its rumble grows as it gains, and the gauge
' shows how far back it is. Slow down too much, fall too often, and it
' catches you. Reach the third village to win.
'
' Controls (either joystick): left and right steer; up tucks (faster);
' down snowploughs (slower). Either button starts and goes through the
' pages. Keypad not used.
'
' What is not from the book: everything but the theme. The book's game is
' a turn-by-turn budget for the mayor; this is a new action game on the
' same mountain, and it names its inspiration on the credits page.
'
' House rules kept: text in columns 2-29 and rows 1-22 (the ground and
' mountains run to the edges, as pictures); six sprites in all, the two ski
' tips (rows 21-23 only) and four objects; the frame loop is protected from
' the NMI, and every screen is drawn with the NMI off after a wait.
'
' How the picture works: rows 8-23 are the ground. Each row uses its own
' four tiles (snow, forest, left edge, right edge) in its third of the
' screen. The four share one colour per pixel line, forest in front and
' snow behind, so one 32-byte copy recolours a row. Every frame each pixel
' line takes its depth from a table, adds the distance run, and turns grey
' or white: the snow bands rush toward you. Every frame each row also gets
' its forest edges from the player's place, the piste's width and the
' curve (which reaches further at a distance); they move in half tiles.

